  "changes": [
    {
      "version": "0",
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "change": "Eight fields from the electronegativity example: task, data_type, views, interactions, pattern, stage, field, sources.",
      "because": "A starting point to describe the first UIs with."
    },
    {
      "version": "1",
      "date": "2026-10-23",
      "change": "Grouped into the five facets on the Architecture board: task (with verbs), data (with shapes and the item), stage, mechanism (views, interactions, patterns), field. data_type became a list of shapes. sources moved to build.json, since it says where a build came from, not what it is. Added words for qualitative coding and paper reviewing (annotate, reconcile, review, rate; spans, categories, judgements; category palette, rubric, review queue, agreement summary).",
      "because": "One vector per facet lets a search weight data shape and task above field. A UI often holds more than one kind of data (transcripts and a codebook). The first examples are qualitative coding and paper reviewing, and v0 had no words for them."
    },
    {
      "version": "2",
      "date": "2026-10-23",
      "change": "field gained `disciplines`, a short list of broad sciences, beside its free `terms`; a card that names none gets one inferred from its terms. Added the interaction `import` and the view `strip plot`.",
      "because": "Free field words could not tell whether two builds come from the same science, so 'human factors' and 'HCI' counted as different fields and the far match crossed nothing. `import` was proposed by two cards; `strip plot` by a card and by a request in the evaluation set."
    },
    {
      "version": "3",
      "date": "2026-10-23",
      "change": "Cards gained a workflow: a general name, ordered steps (each a general step, how this build does it, and the parts that do it), and what is distinctive about this case. Search matches on the general workflow and then compares the steps and specifics; `astaui workflows` groups builds into workflow families.",
      "because": "Builds are filed through the workflow they serve. The facets said what a build is, but not the order of the work, and nothing said how two builds with the same workflow differ."
    }
  ],
  "workflow": {
    "says": "The job as a sequence of steps. Each step is said twice: in general words any science could use (one of the moves below when one fits), and as this build does it. Then what is particular to this case.",
    "why": "Two builds can share a workflow and still differ in the specifics that matter to their scientists. Matching on the general steps finds the shared workflow; comparing the specifics and the distinctive needs says how each case is its own.",
    "weight": 0.25,
    "moves": {
      "bring the data in": "Load, paste or import what will be worked on.",
      "look over every item at a glance": "An overview of the whole collection at once.",
      "open one item in detail": "Everything about one item, close up.",
      "step through items one at a time": "A queue that moves on after each item.",
      "select a stretch and label it with a category": "Mark part of a text, sequence or signal and apply a code or label.",
      "decide keep or drop for each item, with a reason": "A keep, reject or maybe call on every item, with why.",
      "score each item against criteria": "Rate or judge an item on fixed criteria.",
      "compare items side by side": "Two or more items, people or versions next to each other.",
      "group items into clusters": "Arrange items into named groups.",
      "filter to the items that matter": "Narrow the collection by a property or a search.",
      "compute a value from the data": "Fit, count, measure or derive something.",
      "exclude bad items and recompute": "Leave items or points out and redo the computation.",
      "find where people disagree and resolve it": "Set judgements by several people against each other and settle them.",
      "track progress toward a target or a total": "Counts, totals and how far along the work is.",
      "keep a record of changes": "Versions, a log or a history of decisions.",
